description:
On 2026-10-09 the repository's Actions queue held about 20 queued runs while only 1 to 3 were in progress. Oracle runs waited for runners at each job (plan, select, attempt, post):
- shadow run 37941584716 for #2820 was queued at 14:05 UTC and posted at about 15:10;
- shadow run 37961549758 for #2831 waited about 2.5 hours;
- the canary #2826 run 37961215694 was queued at 16:43 and posted at 18:04.
A full oracle round takes a few minutes of runner time, so almost all of the wait was queueing. Each oracle run is several sequential jobs, which multiplies queue time, and CI on every PR competes for the same runners.

Known facts: the organisation BenchBox-dev is on the GitHub free plan and the repository is public (standard runners are free, with a concurrent-job cap). One CI run (37995560304) has 39 jobs. There are no self-hosted runners. The org's runners are shared with the benchbox-site repository. Hypothesis to confirm: the free-plan concurrent-job limit (documented as 20 jobs) is the bottleneck. Measure in jobs, not runs: a single CI run has more jobs than the limit, so a few concurrent runs can fill every slot.

Slot consumers to count:
- oracle-verdict jobs (plan, select, attempt, post per round);
- review-event churn (oracle-review runs started by thread replies; see oracle-review-stale-check-runs-block-merge-20261008);
- codex quota retries;
- Nightly T3 dispatches (nightly-v2.yml);
- CI fan-out on each push, and benchbox-site workflows.

Investigate:
- confirm the concurrent-job limit and usage (plan, minutes, other repositories);
- which workflows dominate the queue, by jobs;
- whether oracle jobs can run as fewer, larger jobs;
- whether CI fan-out on each push can be reduced.
Report numbers before proposing a change.

Guardrails. Investigation first; any workflow change that touches the oracle is very-high tier and goes through the normal oracle PR flow. Do not weaken required checks to save time.
